# Commit messages (Conventional Commits)

When the user asks you to **draft, suggest, or write a git commit message**, follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) unless they specify otherwise.

## Format

```
<type>[optional scope][optional !]: <description>
```

- **Subject line**: imperative mood (“add” not “added”), no trailing period, ≤72 characters (aim for ≤50).

## Types

| Type       | Use when |
|------------|----------|
| `feat`     | New user-facing behavior or capability |
| `fix`      | Bug fix |
| `docs`     | Documentation only |
| `style`    | Formatting, whitespace; no logic change |
| `perf`     | Performance improvement |
| `refactor` | Code change that is neither feat nor fix |
| `test`     | Tests only |
| `build`    | Build system or dependencies (e.g. Maven, Docker) |
| `ci`       | CI configuration and scripts |
| `chore`    | Maintenance that doesn’t fit above (tooling, misc) |
| `revert`   | Reverts a prior commit; subject should mention reverted SHA or summary |

Do not invent other types unless the user requests a custom scheme.

## Scope (optional)

Use a short, stable noun: feature or area name (e.g. `oauth2`, `postgres`, `security`, `websocket`, `config`). Omit scope when the change spans many areas or scope adds no clarity. Lowercase; no spaces.

## Breaking changes

- Append `!` after type/scope: `feat(api)!: remove legacy status endpoint`
- And/or footer: `BREAKING CHANGE: <what changed and how to migrate>`

## Footers

- **Issues**: `Closes #123`, `Fixes #456` (when applicable).
- **Breaking**: `BREAKING CHANGE: ...` as above.
- Match existing project trailer style if present in recent history.

## Do not

- Commit secrets, credentials, or `.env` with real values.
- Use vague subjects: “fix stuff”, “updates”, “WIP”.
- Repeat the diff in the subject; one clear outcome per commit.
- Combine unrelated changes in one message; suggest splitting when needed.

## Examples

**Good**

```
feat(oauth2): add client registration use case

Expose registration through the inbound port so adapters stay thin
and domain rules live in application services.
```

```
fix(postgres): handle probe timeout as degraded status

Previously an SQLException surfaced as 500; callers now get a
consistent health payload.
```

```
refactor(security): align filters with hexagonal inbound adapters
```

```
chore(deps): bump spring-boot-starter-parent to 3.4.2
```

**Avoid**

```
Fixed bug
Updated files
Refactor PostgreSQL and OAuth2 features to align with hexagonal architecture. Introduce new use cases...
```

(Long single-line subjects and past-tense/generic titles.)

## When committing for the user

1. Inspect staged changes (`git diff --staged`) and match **one logical change** per commit.
2. Propose a subject that passes the rules above; add body only when it clarifies **why** or migration notes.
3. Prefer the repository’s recent tone for body length; default to conventional subject + short body when the change is non-trivial.
